                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_focus:         `<strong>That's the binding constraint.</strong> The €230,000 gap is the symptom; the disease is three half-funded models burning cash while the founder and team run past their limits. Fix focus and sustainability and the round becomes closeable. Solve only the cash and you'll be back here in five months.`,
                    coach_r1_cash:          `<strong>You've named the symptom, not the disease.</strong> Find the €230,000 and you still have three models, a 70-hour founder and two technicians about to walk. The gap is what's visible; the constraint is the unfocused, unsustainable venture underneath it.`,
                    coach_r1_sales:         `<strong>Growing into the loss is how you got here.</strong> Reviva over-invested in capacity ahead of demand. Pushing more resale volume on weekend shifts accelerates the burn and the attrition — it doesn't close the round.`,
                    coach_r1_valuation:     `<strong>Holding out for a better valuation burns the one thing you don't have: time.</strong> With ~5 months of runway and ~40% of soft commitments evaporating if the round stalls, fighting the €3.0M is how you end up in a distressed down-round.`,
                    coach_r1_runway:        `<strong>Check the arithmetic.</strong> €350,000 ÷ €70,000/month is about five months — not what you entered. The whole urgency of this case rests on that number; get it wrong and every downstream decision is mis-sized.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the real constraint — focus and sustainability — AND ranked the own-brand inclusive-resale core first. That's the model that already earns the 28% margin and carries the mission. Now you can build a plan instead of hedging.`,
                    coach_r1_allthree:      `<strong>Ranking all three equally is the optionality trap.</strong> You can't fund three businesses with eleven people and five months of runway. Refusing to choose isn't flexibility — it's the thing that makes the round un-fundable.`,
                    coach_r1_inclusionlow:  `<strong>If inclusion is barely in the model, it's a slide.</strong> Your five returning-to-work technicians either anchor your unit economics and certification or they're decoration the first margin squeeze will cut. An impact fund will see the difference instantly.`,

                    coach_r2_ownbrand:      `<strong>Focus is the strategic act.</strong> Betting the company on the own-brand inclusive-resale core protects the 28% margin and the mission, and gives the lead fund one coherent story to underwrite. The other two models can wait.`,
                    coach_r2_whitelabel:    `<strong>White-label can scale, but it dilutes the margin and the mission.</strong> Refurbishment-as-a-service for retailers is higher volume and thinner margin — and the women technicians become a cost line, not the point. A defensible choice, but know what you traded.`,
                    coach_r2_subscription:  `<strong>Repair-subscription is the most capital-hungry bet.</strong> Recurring revenue is attractive, but it's unproven at Reviva and it's exactly the optionality you can't afford to fund now. You've chosen the hardest model to reach the close on.`,
                    coach_r2_hedge:         `<strong>That's still two businesses.</strong> Running own-brand and white-label in parallel is the optionality paralysis the lead fund explicitly rejected. Eleven people can't do both well — pick one and the round becomes investable.`,
                    coach_r2_negotiate:     `<strong>You priced control, not just cash.</strong> Taking the +€250,000 with a mission lock and a longer runway on the CEO clause closes the round AND keeps Camille leading the venture she built. That's how a mission-driven founder reads a term sheet.`,
                    coach_r2_loan:          `<strong>Non-dilutive money protects the founder.</strong> The €150k BPI France loan plus a small angel top-up reaches the close while keeping Camille's stake high — at the cost of debt service that the burn now has to carry. A disciplined, control-preserving stack.`,
                    coach_r2_ceoclause:     `<strong>You solved the gap and signed away the CEO seat.</strong> Reviva exists because a corporate parent wouldn't let Camille scale her idea — and you've just recreated that, with an investor holding the exit. Was the €250,000 worth the founder and the mission, with nothing negotiated in return?`,
                    coach_r2_cut:           `<strong>You protected control but the round doesn't close.</strong> A small angel bridge and a burn cut keep Camille's stake intact — but you're still well short of €750,000, the soft commitments are evaporating, and you're betting the next raise lands before the runway ends.`,
                    coach_r2_nocontrol:     `<strong>You took the cash and left control on the table.</strong> Accepting dilution without negotiating governance or a mission lock is how founders — disproportionately women founders — lose the company they built. Price the control.`,

                    coach_r3_hire:          `<strong>You funded the people who produce the margin.</strong> Two technicians at €2,400/month each kill the weekend shift that was driving your best people out. The cost is real and it's in the burn — that's what makes the sustainability plan credible instead of a wish.`,
                    coach_r3_automate:      `<strong>Automation helps, but it's capex before relief.</strong> Automating intake reduces hands needed later, yet the weekend shift bites now and the technicians warned you now. Pair it with protected time or you may lose them before the payback lands.`,
                    coach_r3_cap:           `<strong>Capping volume protects people but slows growth.</strong> It removes the attrition driver and is honest about scarcity — but it caps the very growth you raised the round to fund. A sustainable, conservative choice the fund will question on ambition.`,
                    coach_r3_status:        `<strong>You built a growth plan on your technicians' backs.</strong> Keep the weekend shifts and the two best technicians walk, refurbished volume drops ~30%, the certification backlog explodes and the margin collapses. Where in your plan did the people who do the refurbishing appear?`,
                    coach_r3_combo:         `<strong>This is the human plan done right.</strong> You removed the attrition driver by hiring AND chose distributed leadership so the venture doesn't depend on one exhausted founder. The team can run, and Camille can survive the scaling.`,
                    coach_r3_owner:         `<strong>Owner-operator doesn't scale — and it breaks the founder.</strong> Keeping Camille's hands on everything at 70 hours with a newborn at home is precisely the unsustainable pattern that gates the venture. What does she stop doing, and who picks it up?`,
                    coach_r3_costless:      `<strong>A target with no euros behind it is a wish.</strong> You set founder hours to ≤50 and absenteeism down — but hired no one, capped no volume, and put nothing in the burn. Who does the work Camille stops doing? Fund the target or it's aspiration.`,

                    coach_r4_coherent:      `<strong>One coherent, consistent case.</strong> Model, money and people fit together — that's exactly what the fund probes for. A focused model with a funded people plan and priced control is investable; that's how you close at €750,000.`,
                    coach_r4_growth:        `<strong>A pure growth deck the fund has seen a hundred times.</strong> Biggest numbers, deferred people plan — and an impact fund reads straight through it to the two technicians about to quit and the founder about to break. Consistency, not bravado, closes this round.`,
                    coach_r4_mission:       `<strong>Mission in the headline, economics in the appendix.</strong> An impact fund wants the mission — but it underwrites unit economics. Lead with how inclusion lives in the cost structure, then the impact lands as substance, not decoration.`,
                    coach_r4_inclmodel:     `<strong>That's the Chair's point made real.</strong> Saying the five technicians are central to your unit economics and certification — not a feel-good slide — is the difference between a strategy and a story. The impact fund can tell instantly, and you just showed it's the model.`,
                    coach_r4_inclslide:     `<strong>You just made inclusion a slide.</strong> 'We employ women returning to work' as a feel-good line tells the fund those technicians are the first cut when margin tightens. If they're central to your economics, that's strategy; if they're a slide, your mission won't survive the first hard quarter.`,
                    coach_r4_revenue:       `<strong>A revenue-only commitment unbalances the venture.</strong> Held to top line alone, you'll be back on weekend shifts within two quarters. The fund asked for a venture that lasts — pick a metric that doesn't quietly re-break the team and the founder.`,
                    coach_r4_raise:         `<strong>Framing success as the next round is a treadmill.</strong> Defining the win as the Series A keeps the founder chasing capital instead of building a durable, profitable, inclusive business. The lead fund wants the venture to work, not just to raise again.`,

